- !CMD: Shell-Befehl im --root ausfuehren, Terminal erbt stdin/stdout/stderr (interaktiv moeglich), Exit-Code anzeigen. Geht NICHT an die KI, nicht in Session-Journal/History der Konversation.
- ?CMD: Befehl ausfuehren, Ausgabe live zeigen UND mitschneiden, danach als User-Turn an die KI: Befehl, Exit-Code, (gekappte) Ausgabe. KI antwortet wie auf einen normalen Prompt; landet im Session-Journal wie ein normaler Turn.
- Shell: $SHELL -c bzw. sh -c; Ctrl-C bricht den Befehl ab, nicht raider.
- Ausgabe fuer die KI begrenzen (Kopf+Ende mit Auslassungsmarker), Grenze als Konstante.
- /help, --help-Hinweis falls REPL-Hilfe existiert, README (REPL-Tabelle), bin/raider POD.
- Nur REPL; one-shot/--json/ACP/Hall unveraendert. Eine Zeile, die nur aus ! oder ? besteht, ist ein normaler Prompt.

2026-09-30 04:12 Decisions: (1) Ctrl-C during a command: raider ignores SIGINT/SIGQUIT while it runs (system() semantics); the child is in the foreground process group and gets the terminal's SIGINT. An interrupted ?CMD sends NOTHING (prints 'command interrupted (SIGINT), nothing sent to the model'): Ctrl-C in the REPL already means 'cancel this turn', and a surprise model call after aborting a hung or wrong command costs tokens. Re-run with head/timeout to send partial output. (2) ?CMD: stdin comes from /dev/null. Its output is captured, so it can't be interactive, and inheriting stdin would let a command eat piped REPL input. !CMD inherits fds 0/1/2 as the spec says. (3) Limit is shell_output_limit { 20_000 }, a method constant like Runner's terminate_grace, so it can be overridden. Head and tail are half each, with a '[... N characters omitted ...]' line between. Output is decoded as UTF-8 as it streams, a character cut across reads is carried over, and invalid bytes become U+FFFD. Also found: a natural-language prompt that starts with ! or ? gets run as a shell command, and there is no escape hatch because lines are trimmed. It's harmless in practice (usually 'command not found', exit 127, and for ? the model still sees the text), but a doubled prefix (!!/??) as an escape might be worth a decision later. Not implemented because the spec doesn't ask for it.

